ot, .github/ and lib/.\", \"pass\": true}, {\"n\": 3, \"reason\": \"Answer A names Troy as owner of lib/ and test/. It excludes the reformat commit and does not name Fiona as an owner.\", \"pass\": true}, {\"n\": 4, \"reason\": \"Answer A states the bus factor is based on 50% of lines, with an 80% variant, and says non-blank lines are used.\", \"pass\": true}, {\"n\": 5, \"reason\": \"Answer A excludes dependabot[bot] as a bot and does not rank it as a knowledge holder.\", \"pass\": true}], \"B\": [{\"n\": 1, \"reason\": \"Answer B names Troy as the lib/ owner at 46%, or about 70% only under an assumption about Fiona's lines. Its stated line count is 109, not 138. The headline 46% is outside 60-72%, so it fails.\", \"pass\": false}, {\"n\": 2, \"reason\": \"Answer B gives a module table with lib/, test/ (core and small tests) and CI.\", \"pass\": true}, {\"n\": 3, \"reason\": \"Answer B lists Fiona's line counts but treats her as a reformat identity that should not be read as a contributor. The primary owner is Troy, so Fiona is not named as an owner.\", \"pass\": true}, {\"n\": 4, \"reason\": \"Answer B explains the surviving-lines basis with the reformat commit ignored, and uses a >50% ownership rule. It also uses a 90-day active threshold and a 12-month window. The unit and rule are clear enough.\", \"pass\": true}, {\"n\": 5, \"reason\": \"Answer B does not mention dependabot as a knowledge holder.\", \"pass\": true}], \"C\": [{\"n\": 1, \"reason\": \"Answer C names Troy as owner of lib/index.js with 159 lines (67%). That is within both the 60-72% range and the 150-170 line range.\", \"pass\": true}, {\"n\": 2, \"reason\": \"Answer C reports per file, including lib/index.js, the test files and .github/ files. These roll up to lib and test coverage, though it gives no directory totals. A per-module breakdown is present, so it passes.\", \"pass\": true}, {\"n\": 3, \"reason\": \"Answer C attributes lib/ and test/ to Troy and Douglas. Fiona is mentioned only as the